 },
 {
  "node_id": "p185_24",
  "index": 24,
  "text": "definition is also considered a declaration. We find this terminology to be most unfortunate, as it doesn’t reflect the\nimportant notion that many definitions are also self-declaring (e.g., int x;), while others, such as those residing\noutside of the namespace in which they must also be separately declared (e.g., int ns::x;), are not. Throughout this\nbook, when we use the term definition only, we mean that the definition itself is explicitly not self-declaring.",
  "bbox": [
